# The @codeflow scope lives on the public npm registry. Pinning it here means a
# contributor whose global npm config points at a private registry still resolves
# (and publishes) these packages against npmjs.org.
@codeflow:registry=https://registry.npmjs.org/

# No credentials here, and deliberately not even the templated form.
#
# npm's own guide for CI/CD tells you to commit the line
# `//registry.npmjs.org/:_authToken=${NPM_TOKEN}` — the token stays in the
# environment, only the placeholder is committed. That advice is sound for npm
# and wrong for a pnpm workspace: when `NPM_TOKEN` is not set, pnpm warns
# "Failed to replace env in config" and then **discards the whole file**, taking
# the `@codeflow:registry` line above down with it. Checked, not assumed:
#
#   with the token line, no NPM_TOKEN → `pnpm config get @codeflow:registry` is `undefined`
#   without it                        → the registry resolves normally
#
# Every contributor and every CI job that does not publish falls in that first
# case, and it fails silently. So the auth line is written by the release
# workflow into `$HOME/.npmrc` instead, in the same templated form, where it
# cannot take anything else with it. See .github/workflows/release.yml.
#
# To publish from a laptop: `npm login`, which writes the token to ~/.npmrc.
